  "query_preview": "..Storms started forming early over high terrain areas \nincluding the Huachuca and White Mountains around 11 am MST which \nwas a bit sooner than convective allowing models suggested. \nAdditional storm activity is anticipated to continue this afternoon \nand evening with the strongest activity mainly focused south and \neast of Tucson. The primary concerns with storms this afternoon \ncontinues to be heavy rainfall (rates 1.0 to 2.5 inches per hour) \nand frequent lightning. However, gusty and erratic outflow winds to \naround 45 mph cannot be ruled out with the strongest storms as well. \nHeavy rainfall may result in isolated instances of flash flooding, \nespecially across Santa Cruz County, southwest Cochise County, and \nfar southeast Pima County. Portions of eastern Santa Cruz county and \nSW Cochise county has seen heavy rain over the past 72 hours, so the \nsoils are wet, and the San Pedro has had some increased flow several \ndays ago making them more susceptible to saturation this afternoon. \nFlowing water can be expected in normally dry washes. Sensitive low \nwater crossings may become impassible with swift moving water. \nRemember to remain alert, avoid driving through flooded roads, and \nobey road closures.\n\nThe extent of showers and storms tomorrow will largely depend on \nhow the evolution of storm activity this afternoon and evening. \nBut even if we see less coverage of storms tomorrow, sufficient \nmoisture in place could again be supportive of heavy rainfall. \nThe 11/12Z HREF depicts a slight chance (10-30%) for heavy rain \nexceeding 2 inches in a 3 hour period across Santa Cruz County \nand southeast portions of Pima County.\n\nSunday through Thursday monsoonal moisture continues to remain in \nplace daily chances (20-50%) for showers and storms. Otherwise, high \ntemperatures will remain near normal through Monday afternoon before \nwe cool off and highs sag slightly below normal.",
